00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:07.340
Welcome back! Vectorization is basically the art of getting rid of explicit for loops in your code.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:15.340
In the deep learning era, certainly in deep learning in practice, you often find yourself training on relatively large datasets,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.340
because that's when deep learning algorithms tend to shine.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:24.340
And so it's important that your code run quickly, because otherwise, if you're training on a big dataset,

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.340
your code might take a long time to run and you just find yourself waiting a very long time to get the result.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:36.340
So in the deep learning era, I think the ability to perform vectorization has become a key skill.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.340
Let's start with an example.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.340
So what is vectorization?

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:48.340
In logistic regression, you needed to compute z equals w transpose x plus b,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:54.340
where w was this column vector and x is also this vector.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.340
Maybe they're very large vectors if you have a lot of features.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:06.340
So w and x were both these Rn or Rnx dimensional vectors.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:15.340
So to compute w transpose x, if you had a non-vectorized implementation,

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:24.340
you would do something like z equals 0 and then for i in range of nx,

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:34.340
so for i equals 1 to nx, z plus equals wi times xi.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.340
And then maybe you do z plus equals b at the end.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.340
So that's a non-vectorized implementation.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.340
And you find that that's going to be really slow.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:49.340
In contrast, a vectorized implementation would just compute w transpose x directly.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:02:03.030
In Python or in NumPy, the command you use for that is z equals np dot w comma x.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:09.030
So this computes w transpose x and you can also just add b to that directly.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.030
And you find that this is much faster.

