00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:40.870
And when you are implementing deep learning algorithms,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.870
you can really get the result back faster and it's much faster if you vectorize your code.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:53.870
So some of you might have heard that a lot of scalable deep learning implementations are done on a GPU on a graphics processor unit.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:58.870
But all the demos I did just now in the Jupyter notebook were actually on a CPU.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:03.870
And it turns out that both GPU and CPUs have parallelization instructions.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.870
They're sometimes called SIMD instructions.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:10.870
This stands for single instruction multiple data.

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:17.870
But what this basically means is that if you use built in functions such as this NP dot function

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:22.870
or other functions that don't require your explicitly implementing a for loop,

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:32.870
it enables it enables Python NumPy to take much better advantage of parallelism to do your computations much faster.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:37.870
And this is true both for computations on CPUs and computations on GPUs.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.870
It's just that GPUs are remarkably good at these SIMD calculations.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.870
But CPUs are actually also not too bad at them.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.870
Maybe just not as good as GPUs.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.870
So you see how vectorization can significantly speed up your code.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:56.870
The rule of thumb to remember is whenever possible, avoid using explicit for loops.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:03.870
